# PAM configuration for wdm — Debian and derivatives.
#
# Install as /etc/pam.d/wdm. The deb package does this; the Arch and Fedora
# stacks live in pam.d-wdm and pam.d-wdm.fedora.
#
# Same shape as the Arch stack, but Debian has no system-login: the common
# stack is split across the pam-auth-update managed common-* fragments, pulled
# in with @include as the other Debian display managers do. common-session is
# what brings in pam_systemd, which creates the logind session and
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR that wdm passes to the launched session; without it most
# desktops will not start. pam_loginuid is not in any common-* fragment, so it
# is listed here the way sddm and gdm list it.
#
# pam_env and pam_limits are in the same position: pam-auth-update generates
# only permit, deny, umask, unix and systemd into the common-* fragments, so
# every Debian login service lists these two itself, and a service that forgets
# them differs from ssh and login without saying so. Without pam_env the
# session gets no /etc/environment and no /etc/default/locale; without
# pam_limits the caps in /etc/security/limits.conf — nproc, maxlogins, memlock,
# nofile — apply on every other way into the machine but not on this one.
#
# ponytail: pam_loginuid is inert as wdm currently forks, and is listed for the
# day that changes rather than for what it does today. loginuid is per-task and
# inherited from the *forking* thread; wdm runs pam_open_session on its PAM
# thread while forking the session from the main one, and fork(2) copies only
# the calling thread, so the session carries wdm's own loginuid and every
# graphical login is unattributable in the audit trail. Being `required` makes
# it look load-bearing; it is not, here. The upgrade path is to fork the session
# from the PAM thread, or to write /proc/self/loginuid in the forking thread
# after pam_open_session returns — noting that with
# AUDIT_FEATURE_LOGINUID_IMMUTABLE the kernel permits that write only once per
# task, which is what makes forking from the PAM thread the better fix. The
# Fedora stack carries the same note, alongside three more modules with the
# same defect.

auth        requisite   pam_nologin.so
auth        required    pam_env.so readenv=1
auth        required    pam_env.so readenv=1 envfile=/etc/default/locale
@include common-auth
-auth       optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so
#-auth      optional    pam_kwallet5.so

@include common-account

@include common-password

session     required    pam_loginuid.so
session     required    pam_limits.so
@include common-session
-session    optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
# pam_kwallet5 is deliberately NOT started here. It forks inside
# pam_sm_open_session and, on the error path, calls exit() in the child rather
# than exec'ing. wdm still holds DRM and a live EGL context at that point, so
# the child runs the graphics driver's atexit handlers against GPU state it
# shares with the parent. On NVIDIA that faults wdm's own channel
# (NVRM: Xid 31 ... name=wdm) and takes the compositor down mid-login: the user
# authenticates successfully and then loses the display manager. pam_gnome_keyring
# forks too but execs, which resets the inherited state, so it is safe and stays.
#
# The `-auth` line above is commented out for the same reason, one step earlier:
# pam_kwallet5's auth module does nothing but stash the password in a pipe for
# the session module to read, so with the session line gone it does work whose
# result nothing collects. The two go and come back together — leaving the auth
# line live would make re-enabling kwallet two half-edits instead of one.
#
# Re-enable both only once wdm runs the PAM session somewhere that holds no
# graphics state. Until then the session line is a crash on any machine with a
# driver whose atexit handlers touch the GPU.
#-session    optional    pam_kwallet5.so auto_start
